# React Query Rules

## Query Client Setup
- Use a proper QueryClient configuration with appropriate defaults.
- Set up consistent staleTime and gcTime across queries.
- Configure error handling and retry logic at the client level.
- Implement proper devtools configuration.

## Query Client Pattern
```typescript
// lib/query/query-provider.tsx
'use client';

import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { ReactQueryDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-query-devtools';
import { useState } from 'react';

export function QueryProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [queryClient] = useState(() => new QueryClient({
    defaultOptions: {
      queries: {
        staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
        gcTime: 10 * 60 * 1000,   // 10 minutes
        retry: 1,
        refetchOnWindowFocus: false,
      },
    },
  }));

  return (
    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
      {children}
      <ReactQueryDevtools />
    </QueryClientProvider>
  );
}
```

## Query Key Structure
- Maintain a centralized query keys file.
- Use consistent and hierarchical query key structure.
- Include relevant parameters in query keys for proper caching.

## Query Key Pattern
```typescript
// lib/query/query-keys.ts
export const queryKeys = {
  entity: {
    all: ['entity'] as const,
    lists: () => [...queryKeys.entity.all, 'list'] as const,
    list: (filters: any) => [...queryKeys.entity.lists(), filters] as const,
    details: () => [...queryKeys.entity.all, 'detail'] as const,
    detail: (id: string) => [...queryKeys.entity.details(), id] as const,
  },
};
```

## Query Hook Pattern
- Create entity-specific query hooks.
- Use consistent naming conventions.
- Implement proper typing for parameters and return values.
- Configure appropriate options for each query.

## Query Hook Example
```typescript
// hooks/entity/use-entity-query.ts
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { queryKeys } from '@/lib/query/query-keys';
import { EntityService } from '@/lib/services/entity-service';
import type { EntityFilters, Entity } from '@/types/entity';

export function useEntity(id: string) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: queryKeys.entity.detail(id),
    queryFn: () => EntityService.getEntity(id),
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
    enabled: !!id,
  });
}

export function useEntities(filters: EntityFilters) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: queryKeys.entity.list(filters),
    queryFn: () => EntityService.getEntities(filters),
    keepPreviousData: true,
  });
}
```

## Mutation Hook Pattern
- Create entity-specific mutation hooks.
- Implement optimistic updates where appropriate.
- Handle success and error cases consistently.
- Update cache appropriately after mutations.

## Mutation Hook Example
```typescript
// hooks/entity/use-entity-mutations.ts
import { useMutation, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { queryKeys } from '@/lib/query/query-keys';
import { EntityService } from '@/lib/services/entity-service';
import type { CreateEntityDto, Entity } from '@/types/entity';
import { toast } from 'sonner';

export function useCreateEntity() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: (data: CreateEntityDto) => 
      EntityService.createEntity(data),
    
    onSuccess: () => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.entity.lists() });
      toast.success('Entity created successfully');
    },
    
    onError: (error: any) => {
      toast.error(`Failed to create entity: ${error.message}`);
    },
  });
}

export function useUpdateEntity() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: ({ id, data }: { id: string; data: Partial<Entity> }) => 
      EntityService.updateEntity(id, data),
    
    onMutate: async ({ id, data }) => {
      await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.entity.detail(id) });
      const previousData = queryClient.getQueryData(queryKeys.entity.detail(id));
      
      queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.entity.detail(id), (old: any) => ({
        ...old,
        ...data,
      }));
      
      return { previousData };
    },
    
    onError: (_, { id }, context: any) => {
      queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.entity.detail(id), context.previousData);
      toast.error('Failed to update entity');
    },
    
    onSettled: (_, __, { id }) => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.entity.detail(id) });
    },
  });
}
```

## Prefetching & Cache Management
- Implement prefetching for anticipated user actions.
- Use query invalidation appropriately.
- Implement proper cache management strategies.
- Use placeholderData for improved loading states.

## Performance Optimization
- Use selective query invalidation to minimize refetches.
- Implement proper pagination and infinite query strategies.
- Configure appropriate staleTime for different types of data.
- Use suspense mode for React 19 compatibility.
- Leverage new React Query v5 features for performance improvements.